You're right. If you just press right, you should just like sort of lean and then fall over.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:23.120
Yeah, exactly.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:24.580
I'm 100% for this.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.340
I think, I think Super Mario Brothers can take a note from track and field.

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:33.040
And that wouldn't make a lot of sense though, to Europeans because, and it probably, this

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:35.100
episode in general, people in Europe are listening.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:36.580
Because they run to the left in Europe?

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:38.860
That is very funny.

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:43.540
No, it's because we're talking about track and field and they're like, well, I don't know

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.800
track and field. I only know track and field in Barcelona.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:50.200
It's literally just added in Barcelona.

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:53.880
It's added in Barcelona and it came out in 1992, but it's the same game.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:58.240
They didn't even add like any Barcelona, Barcelona architecture?

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:00.680
No, it's the same. Well, maybe in the title screen.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.640
Okay. The title screen changed, but like, no, otherwise it's the same game.

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:06.540
Wow. It's just finally in 1992, they get a taste of it.

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:12.340
Wow. But that was because of course the summer Olympic games of 1992 were in Barcelona.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:14.340
Okay. So it just seemed timely.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:15.520
It was like, oh, they never got this game.

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:21.380
Give it to them now. So, uh, you might think that that's all I have to say about track and field.

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:24.340
I hope. But I found, I found interviews.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:28.600
Oh no. Are you ready for some great developer interview quotes?

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:30.120
Please. Okay.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.940
This is on the development of track and field.

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:37.780
Oh, you want to know why we made track and field.

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:39.180
It's simple enough.

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:44.860
We were inspired by the 1984 LA Olympics held last year and said to each other.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.500
Yeah, let's make a sports game like this.

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:52.460
Indeed. There had not been a track and field style athletic game of event game yet.

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:54.320
Lies. We just went over that.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:00.020
We also wanted to see if we could create a game that wasn't human verse CPU, but human

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:05.820
versus human. I think that Pong was one of the earliest video games.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:07.320
Was human versus human.

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:10.000
Keep in mind that there is a human versus human mode.

00:31:10.580 --> 00:31:13.780
Sure. But it's weird that we're talking like that.

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.380
Yeah. There is a human versus human mode.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:19.700
Um, after deciding on our path, we got to work.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:25.060
Every morning we'd grab our fresh, warm bento lunch boxes and head down to the local college

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:30.140
track and field to watch them compete and get a real feel for how it all works.

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:34.580
Oh, so they're just mashing their feet into the ground.

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:36.880
Like we could just mash these buttons.

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:42.940
Okay. We also watched and researched videos from the Tokyo Olympics and other events featuring Olympic

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:51.040
athletes. One of our developers adored the Finnish javelin thrower Tiana Lealek so much as he, so much.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:56.740
He insisted that we add javelin to the events and thus javelin was given to track and field.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:00.340
That is, you know, basically.

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:02.800
That's the story. That's the story of why they made it.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:06.580
Okay. It seems like you could have given that same answer.

00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:07.780
I know. And I prompted you.

00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:11.420
They seem to be taking credit for some very basic inferences.

00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:14.220
Like we wanted to make a game that.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:16.360
Involved track and field events.

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:19.380
No, no, no. Just like, you know, that humans play.

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:23.700
I like making games that humans play against each other.

00:32:24.920 --> 00:32:26.920
Anyway, another one for you here.

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.420
If you're willing to listen to one more.

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:31.760
If the listener is.

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:37.680
Okay. My first game, track and field, took half a year with a grand total of two people working

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:38.920
on programming and design.

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:41.600
I think that shows by the small limitations.

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:46.140
Mm-hmm. I don't think Gradius took even half a year with a team of four people.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:51.440
We did spend time developing a special controller for track and field because people complained

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:52.880
the regular one hurt, however.

00:32:53.160 --> 00:32:54.460
And I'll try.

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:56.680
I saw this once, but I can't find it anymore.

00:32:56.840 --> 00:32:58.240
I'll try to link this in the show notes.

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:01.440
It's a, it's like a box just with one button on each side.

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:05.680
For those who, you know, for playing the alternating style.

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:06.980
Which the NES doesn't have.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:11.280
And then he just goes on a tangent about Gradius, which I feel like we love Gradius.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:12.960
Well, I'll always listen to Gradius stuff.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:14.880
Yeah. So here's a Gradius thing about him.

00:33:14.920 --> 00:33:16.020
This is now a Gradius episode.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:20.320
Gradius was something we were ordered to port, so we tried to be realistic about it.

00:33:20.440 --> 00:33:25.880
The number of sprites on the NES is overwhelmingly small, so we had no illusions about what we

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:26.700
were capable of.

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:32.060
Laughs. We just said to ourselves, well, that's as much as we can do, and left the game at that.

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:36.820
I had one guy under me, and he played through the coin-op version.

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:38.200
That one's really tough.

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:41.040
I didn't play that much, and obviously couldn't beat it myself.

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:42.600
So I put in the Konami code.

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:45.120
The developer for the game.

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:50.320
The developer of the NES port of Gradius was like, I didn't play the game much.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:53.500
And it doesn't take much to make these games.

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:55.080
You just gotta fix them.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:56.420
Oh my god.

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:00.380
I think this is so funny that they're just so casual about these things.

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:02.960
What a cool environment to work in back then.

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:07.560
Now Konami's just looking at numbers and pachinko machines and being like, cool.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:10.240
Can we take a moment of silence for Konami?

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:12.840
A moment of silence for Silent Hills as well.

